From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <adyoull@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 23:36:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SDL2 client: faster screen updates, mouse wheel, resolution
 changes

From the riscos2test report on a Pi 4: slow, the scroll wheel did
nothing and changing the resolution had no effect.

Screen updates (graphics.c; always on RISC OS, elsewhere with
-DFREECIV_SDL2_DIRECT_PRESENT):
- Upstream uploads the whole main surface to a texture and copies all
  of it through the renderer for every update, even a button or the
  mouse pointer. On RISC OS that is a software copy with colour
  conversion plus a sprite plot of the whole window each time.
- Now there is no renderer: the main surface has the window
  framebuffer's colour order (plus alpha, which create_surf() copies),
  screen_blit() records the areas it changes, and update_main_screen()
  copies just those into SDL_GetWindowSurface() and shows them with
  SDL_UpdateWindowSurfaceRects() (a Wimp_UpdateWindow per area; over 32
  areas, their bounding box).
- Checked on Linux (X11): the same pixels as upstream on the pregame
  page, and the game, dialogs and scrolling look right.

Mouse wheel (gui_main.c, widget_scrollbar.c, widget.c): upstream's SDL2
client ignored SDL_MOUSEWHEEL. Over a dialog with a scrolled list (the
standard scroll buttons) it scrolls the list, 2 rows a step; over the
map it scrolls the map, a quarter of the view a step (Shift or a
sideways wheel: left and right).

Resolution (gui_main.c, optiondlg.c): changing "Screen resolution"
only moved the widgets; the window size changed only after a restart.
It now resizes the window and its surfaces at once, as the full screen
option does. On RISC OS, in a window, the list offers the usual window
sizes that fit the desktop (the display driver lists few modes).

Log (RISC OS, with --debug 3): every 10 s, how busy the client was and
how many screen updates, pixels and milliseconds showing them took.
